WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.013
Summer, was passing too quickly and although he was having fun with the girls, Laurie felt restless.

2
00:00:07.014 --> 00:00:10.201
He wanted to travel and visit his friends in Europe.

3
00:00:10.202 --> 00:00:17.126
Thinking about this, he sat on the window ledge, looking across at his unusually quiet neighbors one day.

4
00:00:17.127 --> 00:00:22.472
"What is she up to?" Laurie wondered as he suddenly caught sight of Jo coming out of the house.

5
00:00:22.473 --> 00:00:27.237
He was about to call out to her when he noticed her acting very strangely.

6
00:00:27.238 --> 00:00:30.775
"Where is that girl going?" he asked himself.

7
00:00:30.776 --> 00:00:37.788
Jo tiptoed out of the house, being careful not to slam the door, and quietly walked down the path.

8
00:00:37.789 --> 00:00:43.457
She was carrying a large envelope, which she held tightly as if her life depended upon it.

9
00:00:43.458 --> 00:00:48.066
She walked briskly down the street, looking behind her every few minutes.

10
00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:53.435
"She's sneaking out! Well, we can't have that!" Laurie chuckled.

11
00:00:53.436 --> 00:01:01.634
He got up and hurried downstairs. Whatever Jo was trying to hide, he was determined to find out.

12
00:01:01.635 --> 00:01:10.846
Laurie found Jo's pace irregular. Sometimes she skipped, but other times she slowed to a crawl as if she were thinking about something.

13
00:01:10.847 --> 00:01:19.239
People like her were the most difficult to follow since you never knew if they would stop suddenly, turn around, and notice they were being followed.

14
00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.803
Still, Laurie thought his disguise was a good one.

15
00:01:22.804 --> 00:01:26.757
He wore his grandfather's hat and walked with a cane.

16
00:01:26.758 --> 00:01:32.875
Jo headed into town, passing the dentist's office then the grocer's, and walked toward the billiard room,

17
00:01:32.876 --> 00:01:39.871
but to Laurie's great relief, she passed that disreputable place and went inside an office located next to it.

18
00:01:39.872 --> 00:01:44.292
"Where on earth is she going?" Laurie was dying to know.

19
00:01:44.293 --> 00:01:53.816
Positioning himself in front of a dress shop, he pretended to be interested in the latest colors of the season, while he waited for Jo to come out.

20
00:01:53.817 --> 00:02:01.972
Laurie wondered how many more times he could raise his hat in greeting to each passing lady, as was the custom for gentlemen to do in those days.

21
00:02:01.973 --> 00:02:09.585
"Good day, Madam," he said, imitating his grandfather's voice as two elderly women approached the shop he was standing in front of.

22
00:02:09.586 --> 00:02:16.372
Keeping one eye on the office where his friend had gone, Laurie politely opened the door for the ladies.

23
00:02:16.373 --> 00:02:25.649
To his delight, across the street, another door was opening at that moment, and soon Jo appeared looking excited, happy, and relieved.

24
00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:29.041
Laurie noted that she was no longer carrying the envelope.

25
00:02:29.042 --> 00:02:34.247
Having successfully ushered the ladies inside the store, Laurie ran toward Jo.

26
00:02:34.248 --> 00:02:38.525
Raising his hat, he shouted, "Excuse me, Miss, one moment, please."

27
00:02:38.526 --> 00:02:43.776
Jo turned around to see a gentleman running (quite quickly, in her opinion) in her direction.

28
00:02:43.777 --> 00:02:47.439
He looked very familiar and his voice sounded like . . .

29
00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.492
"Laurie?" she asked, surprised to see her friend in town.

30
00:02:51.493 --> 00:02:54.015
"Of course it's me," he replied.

31
00:02:54.016 --> 00:03:00.207
"What are you doing here?" Jo asked as she pulled the hat from his hand and tried it on herself.

32
00:03:00.208 --> 00:03:05.343
"Never mind me, Jo March. What are you hiding?" Laurie demanded to know.

33
00:03:05.344 --> 00:03:10.514
Flushing, Jo looked away and shrugged, "I don't know what you're talking about."

34
00:03:10.515 --> 00:03:12.841
"Don't deny it, Jo, I saw you.

35
00:03:12.842 --> 00:03:17.032
I followed you here, so I know you went inside that office.

36
00:03:17.033 --> 00:03:21.907
What is it? What is your secret? Tell me!" Laurie nearly shouted.

37
00:03:21.908 --> 00:03:28.268
Jo crossed her arms stubbornly and said, "That's very naughty of you to follow me, Theodore Laurence.

38
00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:31.700
And I don't have to tell you anything if I don't want to."

39
00:03:31.701 --> 00:03:40.237
Laurie chewed on his lower lip then with a twinkle in his eye said, "If you tell me your secret, I'll tell you mine."

40
00:03:40.238 --> 00:03:43.249
Jo considered his offer then stuck out her hand.

41
00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:46.915
"Deal, but you must promise not to tell a soul."

42
00:03:46.916 --> 00:03:49.827
Laurie swore he wouldn't say a word.

43
00:03:49.828 --> 00:03:58.467
Rubbing her hands together, Jo said breathlessly, "I sold two stories to the newspaper. They bought my stories, Laurie!"

44
00:03:58.468 --> 00:04:04.237
Laurie stared at his friend, then let out a loud shout of joy and gave her a big hug.

45
00:04:04.238 --> 00:04:09.415
The two women who had entered the store now came out and seeing this public display of affection,

46
00:04:09.416 --> 00:04:13.016
covered their mouths and shook their heads disapprovingly.

47
00:04:13.017 --> 00:04:21.927
Ignoring them, Laurie and Jo linked arms and ran down the street, still cheering and making a lot of noise.

48
00:04:21.928 --> 00:04:28.608
As they got closer to their neighborhood, Jo turned to Laurie and said, "Now you have to tell me your secret."

49
00:04:28.609 --> 00:04:32.730
Laurie covered his mouth and held back a giggle. "I don't know . . ."

50
00:04:32.731 --> 00:04:38.961
"Laurie! We had a deal!" Jo said, grabbing his arm. "What is it? Tell me!"

51
00:04:38.962 --> 00:04:45.060
"Okay, but remember, you mustn't repeat what I'm about to tell you," he said seriously.

52
00:04:45.061 --> 00:04:49.258
"Laurie, tell me already!" Jo nearly screamed.

53
00:04:49.259 --> 00:04:53.214
"Brookes has a crush on . . ." Laurie's voice drifted off.

54
00:04:53.215 --> 00:04:59.086
Jo, her eyes growing bigger and more alarmed, whispered, "Who? Meg?"

55
00:04:59.087 --> 00:05:06.375
Laurie nodded, grinning. "It's more than a crush, actually; Brookes is in love with your sister."

56
00:05:06.376 --> 00:05:12.821
Jo stopped, her feet suddenly heavy and heart weighed down with this unexpected revelation.

57
00:05:12.822 --> 00:05:17.066
"No, that's impossible." Jo shook her head.

58
00:05:17.067 --> 00:05:23.329
"Why? Meg is a very beautiful girl and Brookes isn't a bad chap," said Laurie.

59
00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:31.929
Although she knew it would happen eventually, Jo refused to believe that one day her sisters would get married and have their own families.

60
00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:37.285
"Laurie, are you sure?" Jo asked, hoping this was one of his pranks.

61
00:05:37.286 --> 00:05:44.984
Laurie, however, nodded and said, "He wrote it in his diary, which happened to be open one day, and in passing, I . . ."

62
00:05:44.985 --> 00:05:50.761
"You read his diary? You little crook!" Jo punched Laurie in the arm.

63
00:05:50.762 --> 00:05:53.599
"I didn't read the whole thing. I just glanced at it.

64
00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:57.975
Ouch! That hurt, Jo," he moaned, rubbing his sore arm.

65
00:05:57.976 --> 00:06:06.270
Jo pushed him farther away; her once light and carefree steps were now burdened with what Laurie had just told her.

66
00:06:06.271 --> 00:06:13.812
Two days later, Mother and all the girls, except for Jo, were sitting quietly in the living room, all occupied with their own work.

67
00:06:13.813 --> 00:06:19.540
Their peaceful afternoon, however, was suddenly interrupted by a noisy commotion in the foyer.

68
00:06:19.541 --> 00:06:23.273
"My word," Mother exclaimed, putting down her sewing.

69
00:06:23.274 --> 00:06:32.830
At that moment, Jo, the cause of the deafening sounds, burst into the room, swinging a newspaper in the air, and dashing around as if she were on fire.

70
00:06:32.831 --> 00:06:38.016
"Jo! What on earth! Calm down, child!" Mother spoke sternly.

71
00:06:38.017 --> 00:06:42.719
"Sorry, Mother," gasped Jo, as she ran past and pecked her mother on the cheek.

72
00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.496
"Jo! What's so exciting about that newspaper?" called out Amy.

73
00:06:47.497 --> 00:06:52.909
Finally Jo collapsed onto the floor next to Beth, who gently took the paper from her.

74
00:06:52.910 --> 00:07:05.789
It was open to a particular page. "Like Mother, Like Daughter," Beth read, "by Josephine March. Jo! This is your story!"

75
00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:10.582
Soon the entire house was filled with loud cheers and a great deal of shouting.

76
00:07:10.583 --> 00:07:18.754
Jo was now a published writer. As everyone came around to give her a hug, she held on more tightly and longer to Meg.

77
00:07:18.755 --> 00:07:25.709
Now Jo's secret was out of the bag but she wondered how much longer she could keep Laurie's secret from being known.

78
00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:29.824
Jo hoped it would be a long, long time.

